# FastF1's HTTP cache. Large, regenerable, and specific to your machine —
# but keep it locally: a warm cache turns an hours-long load into minutes.
.fastf1cache/

# Car telemetry dumps from tools/fastf1_load.py --telemetry-parquet.
# Hundreds of MB per race weekend. Never commit these.
*.parquet

# The Parquet bundle tools/parquet_export.py writes, and the directory it
# writes into. Regenerable from f1.db in about a second, published as a
# release asset, and 41 binary files that do not delta-compress - the same
# reason f1_database.json is not kept here either.
/parquet/
f1-parquet.zip
telemetry/
out/

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Working copies of the database. The committed f1.db is the built artefact;
# these are scratch.
f1.db-journal
f1.db-wal
f1.db-shm
*.db.bak

# The web front end.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/

# Staged by web/scripts/prepare-assets.js before every dev run and build: the
# database from the repository root, its gzip, a manifest describing both, and
# the wasm from the installed sql.js. Committing any of them would mean a
# second copy that can drift from the first.
web/public/

# tools/f1db_fetch.py clones F1DB here. Regenerable; the generated
# harvest/*.txt files are the committed artefact, not the checkout.
.f1dbcache/

# tools/ergast_load.py --from-dump caches the Jolpica zip here. Regenerable,
# ~14 MB, and CC BY-NC-SA: never commit it.
.jolpicadump/

# The full JSON export. It is a pure function of f1.db and regenerates in about
# a second (`make export`), and at 21 MB it does not delta-compress: every
# refresh that changed a row wrote a whole new copy into history. Four commits
# in one session took .git from 13 MB to 34 MB, almost all of it this file and
# f1.db, and the harvest refreshes weekly.
#
# It is published as a release asset instead, so anything consuming it still
# has a stable URL to fetch. f1_compat.json stays committed: it is the v1 key
# layout other things were written against, it is 109 KB, and being able to
# diff it is the point.
f1_database.json
